7. Why is it dangerous to give 777 permissions to a file? Explain with an example. (C01)
Giving a file 777 permissions (rwxrwxrwx) means that everyone (owner, group, and all others) has Read, Write, and Execute permissions.
Danger: If a file contains sensitive data (like a configuration file with passwords) and has 777 permissions, any user on the system or any process they run can read the sensitive data (r). More dangerously, they can modify (w) or replace the file, potentially introducing malicious code or causing system instability.
Example: A system script, say system_update.sh, is set to 777. A malicious user can overwrite its content with a command to delete the home directories of other users, and when the original script is run by the system, it executes the malicious code instead.
